Q: How to freeze nested objects in JavaScript? A: <pre><code>function deepFreeze (o) {
  Object.freeze(o);
  if (o === undefined) {
    return o;
  }

  Object.getOwnPropertyNames(o).forEach(function (prop) {
    if (o[prop] !== null
    &amp;&amp; (typeof o[prop] === &quot;object&quot; || typeof o[prop] === &quot;function&quot;)
    &amp;&amp; !Object.isFrozen(o[prop])) {
      deepFreeze(o[prop]);
    }
  });

  return o;
};
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/deep-freeze" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.npmjs.com/package/deep-freeze</a></p>
<p>It's public domain so you don't have to give credit :D</p>

Q: How to differentiate context for a clipboard button in a dynamic DataTemplate? A: <p>There are many ways to approach your problem.</p>
<p>I want you to see this, which is part of the uranium UI docs:</p>
<p><a href="https://enisn-projects.io/docs/en/uranium/latest/themes/material/components/DataGrid" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://enisn-projects.io/docs/en/uranium/latest/themes/material/components/DataGrid</a></p>
<p>Specifically this part:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!-- This is a custom column without a property. --&gt;
        &lt;material:DataGridColumn Title=&quot;Actions&quot; &gt;
            &lt;material:DataGridColumn.CellItemTemplate&gt;
                &lt;DataTemplate&gt;
                    &lt;Button Text=&quot;Remove&quot;
                            Command=&quot;{Binding Source={x:Reference page}, Path=BindingContext.RemoveItemCommand}&quot;
                            CommandParameter=&quot;{Binding .}&quot;/&gt;
                &lt;/DataTemplate&gt;
            &lt;/material:DataGridColumn.CellItemTemplate&gt;
        &lt;/material:DataGridColumn&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Notice how the command resides not on the item level, but on your binding context level.</p>
<p>The item is a model, and contains data.<br />
(In the example - name, age, id of the student)</p>
<p>The Binding context has the command, that accepts the model as a parameter.</p>
<p>Now comes the heavy opinion based part:</p>
<p>I do not think you should do anything else but this.</p>
<p>This button needs item data and you are lucky to have clipboard service available to you with some global property. Tomorrow you will need the whole grid data in code, and you will need other services. This means you will be injecting into your items code, and have your business logic separated in two different files. It makes more sense to keep your item data as a plain model, and have the injection done and all the business logic on the outer level. And if you try to coordinate execution of those item-level commands, notify outer properties changes, and so on, you will have to write quite a lot of extra code.</p>
<p>I can continue endlessly on the subject, but it will be pointless.</p>
<p>Just consider that there are very good reasons that this example is written like that, and not some other way.</p>
